<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">LLM agents that compute the right answer can still answer wrongly. Agent harnesses increasingly keep long tool outputs out of the context window, saving them to files that the agent searches or reads on demand. We ask where this costs accuracy with a real scientific tool: PHREEQC, a widely used geochemistry simulator from the USGS, whose reports reach 846.8k characters and whose determinism lets every printed value be traced. Agents use it to answer 158 execution-verified multiple-choice items. When agents read the simulator’s report through a section index rather than in context, GPT-5.1 and GPT-5.2 lose 11.3 and 7.9 points of accuracy, while Claude Opus 4.6 and Claude Sonnet 4.6 show no detectable cost. Re-running all 11,007 logged simulator calls shows that both GPT models compute the keyed value about as often as before; what changes is the evidence they answer from. Under retrieval they often skip the report, answer from their own higher-precision output files, and misround to a neighboring option, a failure we call <em id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">evidence substitution</em>. With the simulation held fixed, retrieval still costs GPT-5.2 9.4 points. For GPT-5.4, more frequent correct simulations mask an evidence-use loss of about ten points, leaving a net change indistinguishable from zero. Naming the authoritative output raises accuracy by 6.3–7.9 points over plain retrieval for the three GPT models; for GPT-5.1 and GPT-5.2, inlining outputs that fit works as well, while asking for correct rounding does not. Context-management choices in agent harnesses should be judged by where accuracy is lost, not only by final accuracy.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para ltx_align_bottom">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center" style="width:345.0pt;"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"></span></p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.26836v1/fig_evidence.svg" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/208;" width="476" height="208"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Same simulation, different evidence (item 121, GPT-5.1). The agent’s simulation writes the keyed value to the report, at the options’ precision, and to its own output file, with more digits. Under TOC the agent reads only its own file and truncates 2.75065 to the distractor 2.750 (evidence substitution), even when told how to round; told to answer from the report, it answers correctly. Right: for GPT-5.1 and GPT-5.2, report skipping over all 158 items, and wrong answers once the keyed value is seen (100 minus closure) on the 137 numeric items.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">LLM agents increasingly call tools whose outputs are long, such as logs, search results, and simulator reports. A harness must choose how such output reaches the model: paste it into the context, truncated to a fixed budget (<em id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">inline</em>), or save it to a file that the agent reads through an outline, a search tool, or ranged reads (<em id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">retrieval</em>). File-based delivery can reduce context usage by loading selected portions on demand <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rajasekaran et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Yet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sen et al. (2026)</a></cite> find that delivering search results as files lowers accuracy in most of the harness–model pairs they test, without identifying why, and models do not always use context that contains everything they need <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xie et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Joren et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. What remains unknown is where the loss arises: a wrong answer rarely shows whether the agent computed the wrong value, never saw the right one, or saw it and still answered wrongly.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A deterministic simulator makes this observable. We study LLM agents operating PHREEQC, an aqueous-geochemistry simulator <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Parkhurst and Appelo, 2013</a>)</cite> whose section-structured reports reach 846.8k characters, on 158 multiple-choice items whose answer keys we re-verified by execution (Section <a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1 Items and Verification ‣ 2 Setting ‣ Same Simulation, Different Evidence: Where Retrieved Tool Output Costs LLM Agents Accuracy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>). We change only how the simulator’s report reaches the agent: inline, or as a table of contents (TOC) of its sections from which the agent reads line ranges.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We re-run every input an agent wrote and search every tool result it received, and so tell for each trajectory whether its simulation produced the keyed value (<em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">build</em>), whether the value reached the agent (<em id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">exposure</em>), and whether the agent then answered correctly from it (<em id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">closure</em>). Each stage points to a different remedy: a build defect to better simulation inputs, an exposure or closure loss to a change in what and how the agent reads. We therefore first locate the loss and then test the location by intervening on that stage. Four results follow.</p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The cost of retrieval depends on the model.</span> Pooled over runs, TOC changes accuracy relative to inline output by <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>0.6 to <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math>0.2 pp for Claude Opus 4.6, Claude Sonnet 4.6, and GPT-5.4, with intervals that include zero, and by <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>11.3 and <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>7.9 pp for GPT-5.1 and GPT-5.2. Keyword search, which uses no simulator-specific structure, does no better (Section <a href="#S3" title="3 The Cost of Retrieval Depends on the Model ‣ Same Simulation, Different Evidence: Where Retrieved Tool Output Costs LLM Agents Accuracy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The loss lies in what agents read, not in what they compute.</span> GPT-5.1 and GPT-5.2 produce the keyed value about equally often under every interface. Under retrieval, however, they often skip the report and answer from their own <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">SELECTED_OUTPUT</span> files, which print more digits, and they truncate such values to a neighboring option in about three of four cases. We call this <em id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">evidence substitution</em> (Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Same Simulation, Different Evidence: Where Retrieved Tool Output Costs LLM Agents Accuracy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). Holding the simulation fixed and changing only the delivery, TOC still costs GPT-5.2 9.4 pp (Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Agents Answer from Their Own Output ‣ Same Simulation, Different Evidence: Where Retrieved Tool Output Costs LLM Agents Accuracy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A null net effect can hide the loss.</span> GPT-5.4 substitutes evidence too and loses about ten points to it on numeric items, but computes the keyed value more often under retrieval, so its net change is indistinguishable from zero.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Intervening on what agents read removes the closure loss.</span> An instruction to answer from the report cuts report skipping to under 2% and raises closure to 99% without changing build; relative to all TOC runs, accuracy rises by <math id="S1.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math>6.3 (GPT-5.1), <math id="S1.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math>7.9 (GPT-5.2), and <math id="S1.I1.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math>7.8 pp (GPT-5.4). Inlining reports that fit has the same effect; an instruction that only asks for correct rounding has none (Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Intervening on What Agents Read ‣ Same Simulation, Different Evidence: Where Retrieved Tool Output Costs LLM Agents Accuracy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Setting</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Items and Verification</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use the four-option multiple-choice items of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2026b)</a></cite>, written by one domain expert. Each describes a PHREEQC simulation setup and asks for one quantity from its output (a pH at a reaction step, a saturation index, a molality in a transport cell); several items share a setup. Reference answers come from PHREEQC 3.8.6 with a fixed, author-supplied <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">phreeqc.dat</span>. For numeric items, distractors typically differ from the key in the last printed digit, so they can be answered only by computing the value, not by estimating it; the remaining items ask for a comparison, a name, or a count.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Answer-key verification.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We re-executed all 200 items of the original pool, using the author’s own input where available (9 of 20 setups) and otherwise a literal encoding of the item text with PHREEQC defaults, never adjusting an input toward the key. An item is retained only if the printed value matches the keyed option exactly and uniquely and does not change under tighter convergence or alternative step-size settings. 158 items from 19 setups qualify. The item author confirmed each of the 42 excluded items as defective (an incorrect key, a numerically unstable redox quantity, or defective wording). Exclusion never used model outputs. All main-text results use the 158 items; Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Item Verification ‣ Same Simulation, Different Evidence: Where Retrieved Tool Output Costs LLM Agents Accuracy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> gives the procedure and shows that the full pool leads to the same conclusions.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Agents and Output Delivery</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each agent has four tools — <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">write_file</span>, <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">read_file</span> (optionally with a line range), <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">list_file</span>, and <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">execute_phreeqc</span> — and works in an isolated workspace for at most 24 steps, committing to an answer by writing it to a file. PHREEQC writes a formatted report (<span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">result.out</span>) that prints each quantity at the precision the answer options use; agents may also add <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">SELECTED_OUTPUT</span> or <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">USER_PUNCH</span> blocks, which write chosen quantities to separate files with more digits. The conditions differ in what <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">execute_phreeqc</span> returns and in the system-prompt line that describes it (Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Prompts, Tool Results, and Conditions ‣ Same Simulation, Different Evidence: Where Retrieved Tool Output Costs LLM Agents Accuracy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>). <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Raw100k</span> (inline) returns the report, head/tail-truncated to 100,000 characters. <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">TOC</span> returns metadata and an index of report sections with starting lines; the agent reads line ranges. <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Grep</span> returns metadata only; the agent finds evidence with a regular-expression search tool. <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hybrid</span> returns the report inline when it fits within 100,000 characters and the TOC otherwise. <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">TOC+Report</span> and <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">TOC+Round</span> add a three-sentence instruction to TOC, to answer from the report or to round to the options’ precision (Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Intervening on What Agents Read ‣ Same Simulation, Different Evidence: Where Retrieved Tool Output Costs LLM Agents Accuracy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate Claude Opus 4.6, Claude Sonnet 4.6, GPT-5.4, GPT-5.2, GPT-5.1, and Gemini 3 Flash Preview through their APIs: the Claude models at temperature 0 without extended thinking, the GPT and Gemini models at API defaults with no reasoning-effort setting. Direct and chain-of-thought (CoT) prompting without tools serve as no-tool baselines.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Runs.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each model has one headline run with Raw100k and one with TOC. We rerun TOC three times for Sonnet and the GPT models and Raw100k once for the GPT models, and run Grep and the interventions (Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Intervening on What Agents Read ‣ Same Simulation, Different Evidence: Where Retrieved Tool Output Costs LLM Agents Accuracy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) on a subset of models; Table <a href="#A2.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ An example trajectory. ‣ Appendix B Prompts, Tool Results, and Conditions ‣ Same Simulation, Different Evidence: Where Retrieved Tool Output Costs LLM Agents Accuracy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> lists every run. For the 33 items whose report exceeds 100k characters, all four TOC runs use one run with a fixed index, as the original index was empty for such reports. Rates are averaged over runs; counts use each trajectory once. Our primary comparison is TOC against inline per model, with every run pooled per item, 95% item-bootstrap intervals, and sign-flip permutation tests Holm-corrected over the five models.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Locating Where an Agent Fails</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For every full-agent trajectory with a complete log (5,773) we rebuilt each input the agent executed and re-ran it offline with the same binary and database: 11,007 simulator calls in all. For numeric trajectories with a last execution available for comparison, re-running that execution reproduces its stored report in 4,971 of 5,009 cases; the rest differ only in line order. For the 137 items with numeric answers we then ask three questions, matching values at the precision the options are printed with:</p>
<ul id="S2.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Build</span> (<math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>): did any of the agent’s own simulations print the keyed value?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Exposure</span> (<math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>): given that it was produced, did the value reach the agent, inline or through a read or search result?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Closure</span> (<math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>): given that the agent saw the value, did it answer correctly?</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Accuracy decomposes exactly as <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\,s\,c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\,s\,c</annotation></semantics></math> plus the accuracy of answers given without the keyed value in view (<em id="S2.SS3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">unsupported</em>: nearest-option picks and guesses). We attribute each condition’s accuracy change relative to the same model’s logged inline run to these four factors with a Shapley decomposition and 95% item-bootstrap intervals; the decomposition allocates an observed difference among the stages and does not estimate independent causal effects. We also record the file from which each seen value came. Matching is numeric, so a printed number counts as the keyed value wherever it appears; automatic and manual stage labels agree on 52 of 58 numeric trajectories read by hand, and the results hold on the 93 numeric items whose keyed value occurs only once in the reference report (Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Failure Localization ‣ Same Simulation, Different Evidence: Where Retrieved Tool Output Costs LLM Agents Accuracy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
