<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">The simulator is necessary.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">With Direct prompting, accuracy is 25.9%–43.0% (Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3 The Cost of Retrieval Depends on the Model ‣ Same Simulation, Different Evidence: Where Retrieved Tool Output Costs LLM Agents Accuracy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), at most about 10 pp above the 32.9% majority-label rate. Simulator access adds 15.8–45.6 pp over the better no-tool baseline for the five Claude and GPT models. Gemini 3 Flash gives no parseable answer on 46% of items even inline (71% under TOC), by exhausting the step budget or by giving its answer only in a chat message (Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Gemini 3 Flash Outcomes under TOC and Raw100k ‣ Same Simulation, Different Evidence: Where Retrieved Tool Output Costs LLM Agents Accuracy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>), so we leave it out of the analyses below.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">GPT-5.1 and GPT-5.2 lose accuracy under retrieval.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Pooling every run per item, TOC changes accuracy relative to inline output by <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>11.3 pp [<math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>18.0, <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>4.9] for GPT-5.1 and <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>7.9 pp [<math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>14.9, <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>0.9] for GPT-5.2 (Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3 The Cost of Retrieval Depends on the Model ‣ Same Simulation, Different Evidence: Where Retrieved Tool Output Costs LLM Agents Accuracy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>; the pool includes the TOC control run of Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Intervening on What Agents Read ‣ Same Simulation, Different Evidence: Where Retrieved Tool Output Costs LLM Agents Accuracy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>). After Holm correction over the five models, the GPT-5.1 difference is significant (<math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.005" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.005</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.005</annotation></semantics></math>) and the GPT-5.2 difference is not (<math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.128</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.128</annotation></semantics></math>; unadjusted <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.032" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.032</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.032</annotation></semantics></math>); with the simulation held fixed, GPT-5.2’s TOC cost is <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>9.4 pp [<math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>14.5, <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>4.6] (Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Agents Answer from Their Own Output ‣ Same Simulation, Different Evidence: Where Retrieved Tool Output Costs LLM Agents Accuracy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>). Resampling the 19 setups instead of items gives intervals of [<math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>19.1, <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>2.9] (GPT-5.1) and [<math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>16.2, <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math>1.0] (GPT-5.2), and leaving out any one setup moves the estimates only within <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>14.0 to <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>9.7 and <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>10.9 to <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>5.8 pp. For both models, the two inline runs score higher than every TOC run and the Grep run (Table <a href="#S3.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ GPT-5.1 and GPT-5.2 lose accuracy under retrieval. ‣ 3 The Cost of Retrieval Depends on the Model ‣ Same Simulation, Different Evidence: Where Retrieved Tool Output Costs LLM Agents Accuracy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). Grep, which uses no PHREEQC section structure, does no better than TOC, so the cost is not specific to the TOC index.</p>
</div>
