<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Claude Opus 4.6, Claude Sonnet 4.6, and GPT-5.4 show no detectable net cost.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Their pooled differences are <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>0.6, <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>0.5, and <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math>0.2 pp, with intervals that include zero; for GPT-5.4 this net result hides offsetting effects (Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Agents Answer from Their Own Output ‣ Same Simulation, Different Evidence: Where Retrieved Tool Output Costs LLM Agents Accuracy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>). In net accuracy the split does not follow vendor lines: GPT-5.4 patterns with the Claude models, and its predecessors GPT-5.1 and GPT-5.2 do not.</p>
</div>
