<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">What the agents compute does not change detectably.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Within each of GPT-5.2 and GPT-5.1, the share of trajectories whose own simulation produces the keyed value (<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>) is similar under every interface (Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4 Agents Answer from Their Own Output ‣ Same Simulation, Different Evidence: Where Retrieved Tool Output Costs LLM Agents Accuracy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), and the build term of the accuracy change stays within 2 pp, with intervals that include zero.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">The loss persists with the simulation held fixed.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To separate delivery from computation, we freeze GPT-5.2’s logged inline run at its first successful execution on 131 numeric items and continue the same visible history twice, once with the report inline and once with the TOC (Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Paired Replay with Computation Held Fixed ‣ Same Simulation, Different Evidence: Where Retrieved Tool Output Costs LLM Agents Accuracy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>). Both arms share the files, system prompt, reading tools, and remaining turns; they can read files and submit an answer but not rerun the simulator. Over three paired repeats, TOC changes accuracy by <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>9.4 pp [<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>14.5, <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>4.6], and the interval also excludes zero when setups are resampled. The loss is as large on the 115 reports that fit inline and on the 84 items whose frozen report contains the keyed value (<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>10.1 and <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>9.9 pp).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">The loss lies in exposure and closure.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For GPT-5.2 under TOC, closure accounts for <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>7.7 of the <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>11.4 pp change on numeric items, with an interval that excludes zero, and exposure for <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>2.5 pp. For GPT-5.1 the loss splits about evenly between exposure and closure, each with an interval that excludes zero (Tables <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4 Agents Answer from Their Own Output ‣ Same Simulation, Different Evidence: Where Retrieved Tool Output Costs LLM Agents Accuracy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and <a href="#A3.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ Produced and seen. ‣ Appendix C Failure Localization ‣ Same Simulation, Different Evidence: Where Retrieved Tool Output Costs LLM Agents Accuracy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>). Grep shows the same pattern. These totals are relative to the single logged inline run and cover numeric items only, so they differ from the pooled contrast in Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3 The Cost of Retrieval Depends on the Model ‣ Same Simulation, Different Evidence: Where Retrieved Tool Output Costs LLM Agents Accuracy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
