<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Under retrieval, agents substitute their own output for the report.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Under TOC, GPT-5.2 and GPT-5.1 receive no part of the simulator’s report in 60.3% and 53.0% of trajectories and most of them read one of their own selected-output files instead (Table <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ The loss lies in exposure and closure. ‣ 4 Agents Answer from Their Own Output ‣ Same Simulation, Different Evidence: Where Retrieved Tool Output Costs LLM Agents Accuracy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>). They define such files about as often inline, but there the report is already in context and they answer from it. We call this <em id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">evidence substitution</em>: answering from output the agent defined itself instead of the tool’s canonical report. The term is descriptive: agents may read these files simply because they are short. Sonnet skips the report in only 10.5% of TOC trajectories.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Answering from one’s own output fails in two ways.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Closure.</em> The report prints each quantity at the precision the options use, whereas selected-output files print more digits, so an agent reading them must round, and the GPT models often truncate instead. When an agent saw a value that rounds to the key but truncates to a neighboring option, GPT-5.2 chose the neighbor in 26/35 TOC trajectories and GPT-5.1 in 19/27, against 1/35 for Sonnet. These cases make up about two thirds of the TOC trajectories that saw the keyed value and still answered wrongly. A typical case reads “gives pH = 2.75065, which rounds to 2.750” for a key of 2.751. Accordingly, answers were nearly always correct when the keyed value was seen as the report prints it, and correct in only 72.5% (GPT-5.2) and 66.3% (GPT-5.1) of trajectories that saw it only with more digits. With both forms in view, 19 of 20 answers were correct; errors concentrate where the more precise form is the only one in view. This comparison is observational, since trajectories of the two kinds can differ in their items; Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Intervening on What Agents Read ‣ Same Simulation, Different Evidence: Where Retrieved Tool Output Costs LLM Agents Accuracy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> tests the link by intervention.
<em id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Exposure.</em> When the keyed value was produced but never seen, the agent had almost always received no part of the report (14 of 17 cases for GPT-5.1, 6 of 7 for GPT-5.2): its own output printed a stand-in quantity, such as a log activity, and the agent answered from it. This is not a failure to look: under TOC, GPT-5.2 and GPT-5.1 take more steps and reads than inline (9.1 versus 7.2 steps), and only one of 30 retrieval losses that we read involved a wrong report section.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px6" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">A null net effect hides the loss in GPT-5.4.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">GPT-5.4 substitutes evidence as well: it skips the report in 41.8% of TOC trajectories and chooses the truncated neighbor in about half of the trap cases (Table <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ The loss lies in exposure and closure. ‣ 4 Agents Answer from Their Own Output ‣ Same Simulation, Different Evidence: Where Retrieved Tool Output Costs LLM Agents Accuracy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>). Relative to its logged inline run, TOC costs it about ten points in exposure and closure (<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>2.6 and <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>7.2 pp), about as much as GPT-5.2, but gains <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math>4.1 [<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math>0.0, <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math>8.1] pp in build, because its simulations produce the keyed value more often under TOC (Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4 Agents Answer from Their Own Output ‣ Same Simulation, Different Evidence: Where Retrieved Tool Output Costs LLM Agents Accuracy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). On this basis (numeric items, three TOC reruns against the logged inline run) the net change is <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>4.4 [<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>12.4, <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math>3.4] pp, and pooled over all runs and items it is <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math>0.2 pp; neither is distinguishable from zero, yet both evidence-use terms are negative with intervals that exclude zero.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
