<td id="S5.T5.4.15.12" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:3.2pt;padding-right:3.2pt;"><math id="S5.T5.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math>2.1</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If the loss arises because agents answer from their own higher-precision files, changing only what they read should remove it without changing what they compute. We test two interventions and one control instruction against a TOC control (Table <a href="#S5.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 5 Intervening on What Agents Read ‣ Same Simulation, Different Evidence: Where Retrieved Tool Output Costs LLM Agents Accuracy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>). <span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">TOC+Report</span> adds three sentences to the TOC tool description and to every tool result: the values in <span id="S5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">result.out</span> are authoritative and printed at the options’ precision; the agent should take the value from the relevant section of <span id="S5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">result.out</span> and not answer from selected-output files, which print unrounded values that can match a neighboring option (verbatim in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Prompts, Tool Results, and Conditions ‣ Same Simulation, Different Evidence: Where Retrieved Tool Output Costs LLM Agents Accuracy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>). <span id="S5.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hybrid</span> adds no instruction and inlines reports that fit within 100,000 characters.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">The instruction changes what agents read and removes the closure loss.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">With the instruction, report skipping falls to 0.0% (GPT-5.2) and 1.6% (GPT-5.1), the agents rarely read their own output files, and 99% of their correct numeric answers that saw the keyed value took it from the report. Closure rises to 98.8% and 99.2%, no trajectory falls into the truncation trap, and build is unchanged within run-to-run variation (Table <a href="#S5.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 5 Intervening on What Agents Read ‣ Same Simulation, Different Evidence: Where Retrieved Tool Output Costs LLM Agents Accuracy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>). Accuracy rises over all five TOC runs by <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math>7.9 and <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math>6.3 pp (<math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math>7.1 pp [<math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math>1.9, <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math>12.4] averaged over the two models) and over the TOC control by <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math>3.5 and <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math>7.9 pp, a comparison limited by one control run per model. GPT-5.2 reaches its inline accuracy; GPT-5.1 remains below it (<math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>5.1 pp [<math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>11.4, <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math>0.9]), with the residual in the build, exposure, and unsupported terms rather than in closure.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">A rounding instruction alone does not help.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To separate the source from the precision step, <span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">TOC+Round</span> replaces the instruction with three sentences that ask the agent to round values with more digits than the options to the nearest value at the options’ precision, never truncating, and name no file. Agents still skip the report about as often as under TOC and closure stays at about 82% (Table <a href="#S5.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 5 Intervening on What Agents Read ‣ Same Simulation, Different Evidence: Where Retrieved Tool Output Costs LLM Agents Accuracy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>); they still choose the truncated neighbor in 17/25 and 14/19 trap cases, although told not to truncate. Accuracy changes by only <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math>0.6 and <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math>1.2 pp relative to all TOC runs, and TOC+Report exceeds TOC+Round by <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math>7.3 pp [<math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math>0.6, <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math>13.9] and <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math>5.1 pp [<math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>1.6, <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math>12.0]. Telling the agents how to round does not stop the misrounding; directing them to the value already printed at the right precision does.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">The instruction also lifts GPT-5.4.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">With the instruction, GPT-5.4 reaches 81.6% in both runs, <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math>7.8 pp [<math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math>2.2, <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math>13.6] above its TOC runs and <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math>7.9 pp [<math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math>2.5, <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math>13.3] above its inline runs. Report skipping falls to 0.0% and closure rises to 99.0%, and the build gain of retrieval remains (<math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math>3.4 pp relative to inline), so the instruction removes the evidence-use loss while keeping what retrieval adds.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Inlining what fits has the same effect.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Over three runs, Hybrid raises accuracy over the TOC control by <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math>4.6 (GPT-5.2) and <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math>8.0 pp (GPT-5.1). Report skipping nearly disappears and closure returns to its inline level (Table <a href="#S5.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 5 Intervening on What Agents Read ‣ Same Simulation, Different Evidence: Where Retrieved Tool Output Costs LLM Agents Accuracy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>). Relative to the inline runs, GPT-5.2 changes by <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math>1.2 pp and GPT-5.1 by <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>5.0 pp, again with GPT-5.1’s residual outside closure.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">What the interventions establish.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Both interventions restore closure without changing build, and for GPT-5.2 the paired replay shows the loss with computation fixed. Together, these results place the cost of retrieval in which evidence the agent answers from. Hybrid needs no knowledge of the tool; the instruction keeps retrieval but must name the canonical output.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Discussion</h2>

<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Retrieval adds a choice of evidence.</h4>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our interfaces are information-equivalent: every number in the inline report is one read away, and for GPT-5.2, with the simulation held fixed, the loss remains on reports that fit inline. What differs is the information a bounded agent can use <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2020</a>)</cite>. Retrieval turns reading the report into a choice of evidence. Inline, one source is in view; under retrieval, the report competes with the files the agent wrote itself, and models that choose badly lose accuracy. The instruction works because it makes that choice for them. This also reconciles our results with work in which retrieval helps weaker models most <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025</a>)</cite>: there a pipeline selects the evidence; here the model does.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Implications for harness design.</h4>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Output delivery should be validated for each model a harness serves, and by where accuracy is lost rather than by final accuracy alone, since a net effect near zero can hide an evidence-use loss. Inlining outputs that fit is a safe default; when agents can create their own outputs, a harness should state which output is authoritative, which in our setting also reduces input tokens.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Delivering tool output to agents.</h4>

<div id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The agent–computer interface, including how files are viewed and searched, changes what software-engineering agents achieve <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Harnesses handle long tool outputs by truncating, masking, or compressing observations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lindenbauer et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, or by writing them to files that the agent opens or searches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Abramovich et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rajasekaran et al., 2025</a>)</cite>; long responses also degrade function calling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kate et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Closest to our work, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sen et al. (2026)</a></cite> find that file-based delivery of search results lowers accuracy in nine of ten harness–model pairs, frontier models included, and hypothesize a read–integrate–retry cycle as the cause. On the items we use, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2026b)</a></cite> report that a table-of-contents interface lowers the accuracy of some models; we verify the answer keys, find the cost to be model-dependent, locate it within individual trajectories, and test the location by intervention.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Sufficient versus usable information.</h4>

<div id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Shannon information does not depend on how a signal is encoded, but the information a computationally bounded observer can use does <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2020</a>)</cite>. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xie et al. (2026)</a></cite> show that messages preserving all decision-relevant information can fail bounded receivers, and decompose the loss into externalization, absorption, and action closure. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Joren et al. (2025)</a></cite> find that smaller models often fail to use sufficient retrieved context, and search agents answer far better from the gold documents than by searching for them <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. A deterministic simulator has no externalization loss, and our exposure and closure stages correspond to absorption and closure.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Locating agent failures.</h4>

<div id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Agent failures are usually attributed by human or model judges <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, or by counterfactual replay that labels data for a learned attributor <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2026a</a>)</cite>; RAG diagnostics separate retriever from generator errors against reference claims <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ru et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Agents for scientific software are usually studied on one package and graded on outcomes: OpenFOAM cases that run <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yue et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, COMSOL targets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mudur et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, or molecular-dynamics subtasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Campbell et al., 2025</a>)</cite>; SUPER checks whether expected outputs appear in executed cells <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bogin et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Re-executing the agent’s own inputs with a deterministic simulator gives every stage a ground-truth answer without a judge. Models prefer self-generated over retrieved context <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tan et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and round to significant digits unreliably <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, which makes the failure we find plausible; we show that an interface choice triggers it.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">On 158 PHREEQC items, retrieving the simulator’s report instead of inlining it costs GPT-5.1 and GPT-5.2 accuracy and shows no detectable net cost for the Claude models and GPT-5.4. Re-executing every logged trajectory shows that the GPT models compute the keyed value but answer from their own higher-precision output and misround it, and that in GPT-5.4 better computation masks this loss; for GPT-5.2, holding the simulation fixed confirms that the loss comes from the delivery. Naming the authoritative output, or inlining reports that fit, removes the closure loss; asking for correct rounding does not. Deterministic tools make such losses visible where final accuracy hides them.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S9" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">9 </span>Limitations</h2>

<section id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Models.</h4>

<div id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All models are closed and accessed through their APIs. Evidence substitution appears in all three GPT models and rarely in Claude Sonnet 4.6, the Claude model with full trajectory logs; we do not determine which model property drives this difference.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Decoding and prompts.</h4>

<div id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Claude agents ran at temperature 0 without extended thinking, and the GPT and Gemini agents at API defaults with no reasoning-effort setting, so we do not know whether the effect persists with explicit reasoning. The full-agent conditions differ in the system-prompt line that describes the tool’s return value, and the two instruction conditions also repeat their instruction in every tool result; the paired replay uses the same prompt in both arms. We test one wording of each instruction.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Runs.</h4>

<div id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Because of the API budget, not every model was run, repeated, or logged in every condition, so some table cells are empty; Table <a href="#A2.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ An example trajectory. ‣ Appendix B Prompts, Tool Results, and Conditions ‣ Same Simulation, Different Evidence: Where Retrieved Tool Output Costs LLM Agents Accuracy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> lists every run. The full-agent conditions were run at different times, and we do not model possible changes in the served models between runs. The four TOC runs share one run on the 33 long-output items and the decomposition uses one logged inline run per model. The paired replay covers GPT-5.2 and starts from checkpoints of its inline run.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">What verification establishes.</h4>

<div id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A retained item’s key is reproduced by PHREEQC under the author’s input or a literal encoding of the item text; this is not an independent scientific review of each question. In three setups (24 retained items) the text does not fully specify the reference input; excluding these items leaves 134 items and the same conclusions (Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Item Verification ‣ Same Simulation, Different Evidence: Where Retrieved Tool Output Costs LLM Agents Accuracy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
