<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Procedure.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Every item in the 200-item author-written pool was re-executed in PHREEQC 3.8.6 with the frozen <span id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">phreeqc.dat</span>. For the 9 setups where the author’s original PHREEQC input was available we ran it unchanged, adding only output statements; for the remaining setups we encoded the item text literally with PHREEQC defaults (mmol/kgw, 25 <sup id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_sup">∘</sup>C, pe 4 unless stated). Inputs were never adjusted toward the key; where nothing matched, a small number of documented alternative readings (step numbering, units, charge balance) were tried and recorded. For redox-sensitive quantities in systems without a redox couple, we re-ran with a tighter convergence tolerance and alternative step-size and kinetic-integrator settings, and marked an item unstable if the printed value changed at the digit that separates the options. An item is retained only if its printed value matches the keyed option exactly and uniquely and is stable. Model results played no part in this process. Table <a href="#A1.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ Procedure. ‣ Appendix A Item Verification ‣ Same Simulation, Different Evidence: Where Retrieved Tool Output Costs LLM Agents Accuracy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> lists the excluded items; the item author reviewed all of them and agreed that each is defective as written.</p>
</div>
