<section id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">The intervention runs.</h4>

<div id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The intervention and TOC control runs used the same harness, item file, and step budget, with the index built for every report size. TOC+Round reached 62.0% and 57.0% for GPT-5.2 and 55.7% and 57.6% for GPT-5.1. TOC+Report reached 67.1% and 66.5% for GPT-5.2, 62.7% and 60.8% for GPT-5.1, and 81.6% and 81.6% for GPT-5.4 in its two runs; GPT-5.4’s logged inline run reached 76.6%. With the instruction, GPT-5.4 defined a selected-output file in only 3.8% of trajectories (83.3% under TOC), and 100.0% of its correct numeric answers that saw the keyed value took it from the report (63.9% under TOC). Of the correct numeric answers that saw the keyed value, the share that saw it in the report rather than only in the agent’s own selected-output files was 40.8% (GPT-5.2) and 68.0% (GPT-5.1) in the TOC control, 99.4% and 99.2% with the instruction, 92.5% and 99.0% under Hybrid, and 98.8% and 100.0% in the inline runs. Accuracy gains under the instruction therefore come from answers taken from the report, not from correctly rounded selected-output values. For GPT-5.2, Hybrid used 59.7k input tokens per item, against 61.8k under TOC and 65.7k inline, with the saving relative to inline coming from long outputs. GPT-5.1’s Hybrid runs used far fewer tokens (28.3k per item), mostly because the agent rarely read an optional database reference file that it read in about two thirds of its other TOC and inline runs; the TOC control run also used fewer tokens (34.2k), so we do not claim this as a saving of Hybrid. Mean input tokens per item under the instruction were 58.0k (control) and 49.7k (instruction) for GPT-5.2, and 34.2k and 30.8k for GPT-5.1.</p>
</div>
