<section id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Reliability.</h4>

<div id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For numeric trajectories with a last execution available for comparison, re-running that execution reproduces its stored <span id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">result.out</span> in 4,971 of 5,009 cases; the rest differ only in line order. The keyed value appears in our reference output for all 137 numeric items; for 115 of them no distractor value appears anywhere in that output, and the main results hold on this subset (GPT-5.2 TOC: closure <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>10.7 pp, exposure <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>3.2 pp; GPT-5.1 TOC: closure <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>5.8 pp, exposure <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>4.2 pp). Because matching is numeric, a printed number could also match the keyed value on a line that reports a different quantity. On the 93 numeric items whose keyed value occurs only once in the reference report, where this is least likely, the TOC terms are closure <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>9.8 [<math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>15.4, <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>4.5] and exposure <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>3.2 [<math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>6.5, <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>0.0] for GPT-5.2, closure <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>6.8 [<math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>11.2, <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>2.9] and exposure <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>4.9 [<math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>8.0, <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>2.5] for GPT-5.1, and closure <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>7.4 [<math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>11.7, <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>3.8] and exposure <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>3.3 [<math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>6.3, <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>0.2] for GPT-5.4 (pp, 95% item-bootstrap intervals). Among correct numeric answers of the Claude and GPT agents, the keyed value was seen in 69–95% of trajectories; the remainder are mostly nearest-option picks when no option value was produced, which we report as unsupported answers. Re-execution moved only 8 trajectories from build to exposure. Of the 1,039 build failures of the Claude and GPT agents, 926 produced none of the four option values, and re-running the agents’ inputs with PHREEQC’s full default printing recovered the keyed value in 5.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Manual audit.</h4>

<div id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Independently of the automatic labels, we read 68 trajectories: 12 TOC losses each for GPT-5.1 and GPT-5.2 (items wrong under TOC but correct inline, each read together with the inline trajectory), 6 Grep losses for GPT-5.2, and 8 Sonnet TOC failures. Seven of the 24 TOC losses were caused by the retrieval channel (the inline run had the value in context, while the TOC run never saw it or misread its own selected output), two were mistakes with the value in hand, and fifteen were build failures, seven of which the inline run also had and answered correctly by choosing the nearest option. Losses selected on the outcome are therefore dominated by build failures that churn between runs and net out, which is why we rely on the decomposition rather than on item-level losses. On the 58 numeric trajectories in this sample, the automatic stage (build, exposure, closure, or correct with the keyed value in view) agrees with the manual one in 52 cases. Five of the six disagreements count a matching number printed for another reaction step or solution as the keyed value, which moves a build failure into exposure or closure (or, once, a lucky answer into a supported one); the sixth misses a value the agent saw only as a logarithm. The sample was selected on outcomes, so this agreement rate is indicative rather than a population estimate. Navigating to the wrong report section occurred once. All eight Sonnet failures were build failures. Representative excerpts: “gives pH = 2.75065, which rounds to 2.750” (key 2.751); “Talc: 23.333 mol … The mineral that dissolved the most is Talc” (final amount read as amount reacted; the inline run with the same input saw the change column).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Answers without the value in view.</h4>

<div id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">GPT-5.1 declines (“none of the options”) in 6.1% of TOC trajectories and 1.9% inline; in 19 of its 22 distinct numeric TOC non-commits the keyed value was never produced. Inline, the same model tends to pick the nearest option instead, which is correct about a third of the time. About half of GPT-5.2’s distinct TOC non-commits (8 of 15) are step-budget exhaustion.</p>
</div>
</section>
