<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Paired Replay with Computation Held Fixed</h2>

<section id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Checkpoints.</h4>

<div id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We take the first execution returning exit code zero in GPT-5.2’s logged inline run, before its result reaches the model. Of the 137 numeric items, 131 have such a checkpoint; the remaining six have no successful execution. Selection uses the execution status, independently of the original final answer or whether the output contains the keyed value. We reconstruct the visible history and workspace by replaying the preceding file writes and PHREEQC calls in order, including earlier failed executions. For the 115 untruncated reports, re-execution matches the recorded report after normalizing path and timing lines and separator rules; the other 16 original returns contain only the capped head and tail. Both delivery arms use the same reconstructed files, verified by hashes.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Paired continuations.</h4>

<div id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Only the pending execution result differs: Raw100k returns the report inline with the usual 100,000-character cap, whereas TOC returns its section index, built at every report size. The arms share a common system prompt, the visible history, file-reading tools, and the remaining budget of <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="24-s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>24</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">24-s</annotation></semantics></math> assistant turns, where <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> is the execution’s turn. They can list or read the frozen files and submit <span id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">final_answer.txt</span>; simulation files remain fixed. We run 3 continuations per item and condition with GPT-5.2’s API defaults and a 4,096-completion-token cap per call. Item order and the order of the two arms within each pair are shuffled with a fixed seed (20261011), and the paired arms run consecutively. The first repeat was inspected before the two additional repeats were run. These 786 continuations are counted separately from the full-agent trajectories in Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Failure Localization ‣ Same Simulation, Different Evidence: Where Retrieved Tool Output Costs LLM Agents Accuracy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
