<section id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Aggregation.</h4>

<div id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each item we average the three paired accuracy differences, then average across the 131 items. A missing submitted answer counts as incorrect; one TOC continuation in repeat 2 exhausts its remaining turns. We compute a percentile 95% interval with 50,000 paired-item bootstrap resamples (seed 20261011), retaining all three repeats of each sampled item. The mean difference is <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>9.4 pp, with interval [<math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>14.5, <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>4.6]. As a sensitivity check for shared setups, we resample the 19 setups with all their items and repeats, retaining the item-weighted mean; the interval is [<math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>13.9, <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>5.2].</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Report size and available values.</h4>

<div id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The 115 frozen reports that fit inline yield 75.4% inline and 65.2% under TOC (<math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>10.1 pp). For the 16 longer reports the corresponding accuracies are 41.7% and 37.5%. Using the matching rule in Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Failure Localization ‣ Same Simulation, Different Evidence: Where Retrieved Tool Output Costs LLM Agents Accuracy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>, the keyed value is present in the frozen report for 84 items; accuracy on these items is 93.7% inline and 83.7% under TOC. The fixed outputs therefore retain the same value while the delivery choice changes how often the agent answers correctly.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Gemini 3 Flash Outcomes under TOC and Raw100k</h2>

<div id="A5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">On the 158 items, Gemini 3 Flash Preview reaches 27.8% under TOC and 50.0% with Raw100k (Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3 The Cost of Retrieval Depends on the Model ‣ Same Simulation, Different Evidence: Where Retrieved Tool Output Costs LLM Agents Accuracy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). Most of its failures are missing answers rather than wrong ones (Table <a href="#A5.T11" title="Table 11 ‣ Appendix E Gemini 3 Flash Outcomes under TOC and Raw100k ‣ Same Simulation, Different Evidence: Where Retrieved Tool Output Costs LLM Agents Accuracy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>). Under TOC, 80 trajectories use up the 24-step budget without committing an answer, against 33 inline; navigating the index takes a median of 8 <span id="A5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">read_file</span> calls per item, against 2 inline. In another 30 (TOC) and 40 (inline) trajectories the agent stops after giving its answer in a chat message instead of writing the answer file. When it does commit a parseable answer, it is correct about as often under both conditions (44 of 46 and 79 of 85), so the gain from inline delivery comes from committing more often, not from answering better. Counts are from the logs of the headline run for each condition.</p>
</div>
